-- title:
--   Theorem 6.1 — law of large numbers for neighborhood empirical measures under strong operator convergence
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a Polish space, $\mu\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{Unif}}([0,1]\times E)$ and $W\in L^1_+[0,1]^2$. For each $n$ let $(U^n_i,X^n_i)_{i=1}^n$ be independent, with $\mathcal L(U^n_i,X^n_i)$ the conditional law of $(U,X)\sim\mu$ given $\{U\in I^n_i\}$. Let $\xi^n$ be $n\times n$ matrices with nonnegative entries and zero diagonal such that $W_{\xi^n}\to W$ in the strong operator topology and
--   $$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac1{n^3}\sum_{i,j=1}^n(\xi^n_{ij})^2=0.\tag{3.9}$$
--   Put $M^n_i=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n\xi^n_{ij}\delta_{X^n_j}$. If $h:[0,1]\times E\times\mathcal M_+(E)\to\mathbb R$ is bounded and measurable and $h(u,x,\cdot)$ is continuous on $\mathcal M_+(E)$ for each fixed $(u,x)\in[0,1]\times E$, then
--   $$\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbb E\big[h(U^n_i,X^n_i,M^n_i)\big]\longrightarrow\mathbb E\big[h(U,X,\mathsf W\mu(U))\big],\qquad(U,X)\sim\mu.\tag{6.3}$$
--
--   This is the general convergence principle behind the approximate-equilibrium results: the neighborhood empirical measure seen by a typical player converges to the graphon measure $\mathsf W\mu(U)$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: (ξⁿᵢⱼ) with values in [0,1] (§6.1, p.24) → ξⁿᵢⱼ ≥ 0, zero diagonal, no upper bound; reason: the proof never uses ξ ≤ 1 (Step 3a needs |φ| ≤ 1, (6.4) needs ξ ≥ 0), and §7.1 applies the theorem to the unbounded ξⁿ of Theorem 3.6; specializes to the printed statement
--   - reading: "(U, X) and (Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ)ⁿᵢ₌₁ are defined on the same probability space and are independent" (p.24) → (Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ)ᵢ mutually independent; reason: Steps 2 and 3a use the independence of (Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ)ⁿᵢ₌₁ explicitly
--   - encoding: (U, X) ∼ μ on the same space, independent of the sample → not carried; the right side is ∫ h(u, x, 𝐖μ(u)) μ(du, dx); reason: the conclusion depends on (U, X) only through μ, and an independent copy can always be adjoined by a product extension
--   - encoding: one sampling for each n → a sampling space that may depend on n; reason: the conclusion involves only the laws at each n
--   - encoding: the Borel σ-field of M₊(E) → Mathlib's measurable structure on FiniteMeasure E (generated by the evaluations); reason: for Polish E it is the Borel σ-field of the weak topology
--   - encoding: Iⁿᵢ = [(i−1)/n, i/n) for i < n and Iⁿₙ = [(n−1)/n, 1] (2.1); §6.1 prints the half-open form for all i → I n i with i : Fin n counted from 0, the last interval closed; reason: (2.1) is the definition; the two forms differ by a null set
--   - encoding: 𝐖m(u) ∈ M₊(E) (2.6) → Wop W m u, set to 0 at the labels u where ∫W(u,v)m(dv,dx) = ∞; reason: for m with uniform first marginal these labels form a Lebesgue-null set (Fubini, p.15), which no expectation over a uniform label sees
-- source:
--   Lacker, Soret, A Label-State Formulation of Stochastic Graphon Games and Approximate Equilibria on Large Networks, arXiv:2204.09642v1, Theorem 6.1 and (6.3), p.25 (setting §6.1, p.24)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GraphonLabelGame_Kernel

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology Set
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace GraphonLabelGame

/-- **Theorem 6.1**. Lacker–Soret, arXiv:2204.09642v1, Theorem 6.1, p.25 (setting of §6.1,
p.24). Let `E` be Polish, `μ ∈ P_Unif([0, 1] × E)`, `W ∈ L¹₊[0, 1]²`, and for each `n` let
`(Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ)_{i ∈ [n]}` be independent with `L(Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ)` the conditional law of `(U, X) ∼ μ`
given `{U ∈ Iⁿᵢ}`. Let `ξⁿ` be nonnegative with zero diagonal, `W_{ξⁿ} → W` in the strong
operator topology, and `(1/n³) Σᵢⱼ (ξⁿᵢⱼ)² → 0` (3.9). Put `Mⁿᵢ := (1/n) Σⱼ ξⁿᵢⱼ δ_{Xⁿⱼ}` (6.2).
If `h : [0, 1] × E × M₊(E) → ℝ` is bounded and measurable with `h(u, x, ·)` continuous on
`M₊(E)` for each `(u, x) ∈ [0, 1] × E`, then
`(1/n) Σᵢ E[h(Uⁿᵢ, Xⁿᵢ, Mⁿᵢ)] → E[h(U, X, 𝐖μ(U))]` (6.3).

**Formalization Note** The paper prints `ξⁿ` "with values in `[0, 1]`" in §6.1; here `ξⁿ` is
only nonnegative (the proof of Theorem 3.6 applies the result to unbounded `ξⁿ`). The sampling
space may change with `n`. `M₊(E)` carries Mathlib's measurable structure on `FiniteMeasure E`
(generated by the evaluations, which for Polish `E` is the Borel σ-field of the weak
topology). -/
theorem theorem_6_1 {E : Type*} [TopologicalSpace E] [PolishSpace E] [MeasurableSpace E]
    [BorelSpace E] (μ : PUnif E) (W : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (hW : IsKernelL1Plus W)
    (ξ : (n : ℕ) → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (hξ : ∀ n, IsInteractionMatrix (ξ n))
    (hSOT : SOTConv (fun n => stepKernel (ξ n)) W)
    (h39 : Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => (1 / (n : ℝ) ^ 3) * ∑ i, ∑ j, (ξ n i j) ^ 2) atTop (𝓝 0))
    (S : ∀ n, Sampling E (μ.1 : Measure (ℝ × E)) n)
    (h : ℝ × E × FiniteMeasure E → ℝ) (hmeas : Measurable h)
    (hbdd : ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ p, |h p| ≤ C)
    (hcont : ∀ u ∈ Icc (0 : ℝ) 1, ∀ x, Continuous fun m => h (u, x, m)) :
    Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => (1 / (n : ℝ)) * ∑ i, ∫ ω,
        h (((S n).Y i ω).1, ((S n).Y i ω).2, nbhdEmp (ξ n) (fun j => ((S n).Y j ω).2) i)
          ∂(S n).P) atTop
      (𝓝 (∫ p, h (p.1, p.2, Wop W (μ.1 : Measure (ℝ × E)) p.1) ∂(μ.1 : Measure (ℝ × E)))) := by sorry

end GraphonLabelGame
